7. Analysis plan: mixed model, interaction, effect sizes, mediation, degradation curves, compute-normalized
8. Power/feasibility: items per task, MDE table, pre-registration rules, declared limitation
9. Falsification criteria per hypothesis
10. Decisions vs external assumptions table
11. Hypothetical compute budget for future execution
12. Limitations & what would change the conclusion
13. Explicit statement of no execution

Keep compact but complete. Include the tables.

Key numbers:
- attended = 512 + k*u; coverage = (k+4)/nblk
- KV cache 32 KiB/token for Llama-3.2-1B-like → 1 GiB @32k
- training cost ~99 GPU-h for 5B tokens @8k; ~188 GPU-h @32k (A100 150 TF effective), plus overhead
- MDE table.

Note E1 iso-budget: at 16k, T_ret=512 only gives 3 valid points (8,64),(16,32),(128,4) — and u=1,2,4 saturate coverage (all blocks retrieved → equals full attention within budget). Important: for compact units, budget 512 lets you read ALL blocks' units at 16k, so it degenerates into "attend everything coarsely" — no lookup decision needed. That means the lookup factor becomes vacuous at small u under E1! Great insight: at 32k, E1 T_ret=512 → u=4 retrieves 128 of 256 blocks (50% coverage), u=1 retrieves 512 > 252 → saturates. So we must choose the operating point so coverage < 100% and lookup is genuinely required: e.g., fix coverage ∈ {8%, 14%, 27%} and set k accordingly, and use T_ret=128 iso-budget (u up to 16 plus 128 with k=1). So the primary comparison should be at fixed COVERAGE (iso-retrieval-decision) with a secondary iso-budget arm, plus the compute-normalized curve. Actually careful: the researcher demanded iso-compute as hard requirement. Solution: primary = iso-budget (hard requirement, E1 at T_ret=128 where all 6 unit levels are feasible and non-saturating), reported jointly with the full (u,k) frontier so we can read both iso-budget and iso-coverage cuts and show the frontier. And explicitly flag: under E1, coverage varies with u, which is intrinsic to InfLLM's design claim (compression buys coverage); therefore the primary contrast measures "compression quality at fixed attention cost" — that is precisely the meaningful version of "is unit representation critical", while the iso-coverage cut measures "information access alone". Then the difference between the two cuts IS the diagnostic of whether U matters via compression quality or via coverage. That's elegant and satisfies both.

Also define U levels concretely:
- u=1: 1 memory token per block (mean-pooled key/value slot, InfLLM-style minimal)
- u=4, u=16: multiple memory tokens per block (sub-unit decomposition)
- u=128 (B): raw block tokens (no compression → equals block-sparse full attention)
Plus orthogonal "content" variants of the unit at fixed u=1: (i) mean-pool value, (ii) last-token value, (iii) extractive summary text tokenized into 1 token? Not possible; instead summary-as-unit uses text tokens (a different budget) → treat "resumen textual explícito" as a separate arm with u=16 text tokens (compressed summary) vs learned memory tokens at u=16. That tests abstraction type at fixed budget. Good — that's the "unit content" sub-factor U2 nested in fixed u.

And SHUFFLED-UNITS control at fixed u.

Lookup factor L:
- L0 none (local window only)
- L1 dynamic top-k similarity (InfLLM-style, softmax over key sims; with/without dynamic softmax normalization variant)
- L2 random-k
- L3 oracle-k (synthetic only)
- L4 external dense/BM25 (real: BM25 substitute declared approximate)
- L5 sequential-prior (last k blocks before local window) — recency prior control
- L6 shuffled-assignment (units permuted)
Plus k sweep.

Interaction of the two "content"/"lookup" — main 2×2 = {L1 vs L0} × {u=16 rich vs u=1 compact} at iso-budget.

Hmm careful: iso-budget T_ret=128 → u=1→k=128, u=16→k=8. Fine at 16k.

Base model decision: Llama-3.2-1B (1.24B, GQA 8 KV heads, head_dim 64, 16 layers, RoPE base 5e... ) — actually Llama 3.2 1B has 128k context and RoPE theta 500000, 16 layers, d=2048, 16 heads, 8 KV heads. Good for our arithmetic. Justify: 1B permits continued pretraining cheaply; native long context lets FULL control run at 32k. Alternative Qwen2.5-1.5B. Declare as design decision (not repo fact), and note weights unavailable in this env.

Seeds: 3 seeds for training (main factor cells at 1B × ~10 conditions... too many models). Important cost note: each (U, unit-content) variant needs its own training run (memory params are learned); L (lookup policy) at inference is mostly a decode-time knob except for train/inference consistency. So model count = f(U, content) × seeds; k and L are eval-time. That reduces cost hugely. Let's quantify: 5 model families (u=1,4,16,128-raw-no-training?, summary-16) × 3 seeds = 12-15 runs × ~100-190 GPU-h = 1200-2800 GPU-h → too much for "small". Offer a two-tier budget: screening tier (1 seed, 2B tokens @8k, 75 GPU-h/run, 5 runs = 375 GPU-h) then confirmation tier (2 extra seeds for the 3 cells that matter = 6 runs × 190 = 1140 GPU-h). Total ~1500 GPU-h hypothetical. State as hypothetical, needs GPUs not present.

Also a much cheaper falsification path: 400M model (Pythia-415M) tier for the interaction with big n. Mention.

Analysis model:
accuracy_ij(item) ~ L*U + length + cov + (1|item) + (1|seed-run); logistic mixed model with item as paired random effect; report OR, η² / partial R², and paired bootstrap CIs over items; for continuous (EM/F1, perplexity) linear mixed model.
Mediation: hit-rate as mediator; use natural-effect estimation? Keep simple: Baron-ish two-step + sensitivity, declared as associational mediation.
Sensitivity slopes: regression of accuracy on log k per u; report slope differences.
Frontier: accuracy vs attended tokens Pareto front per u; AUC of frontier = "representation efficiency" scalar — nice single-number answer to "how critical is U".

Falsification criteria (per hypothesis):
H1 (lookup critical): mean Δ(L1−L0) ≥ 10 pts on NIAH/multi-hop at ≥16k and CI excl. 0; else "lookup not critical" (equivalence declared if 90% CI within ±3 pts).
H2 (unit critical at iso-budget): Δ(u=16 − u=1) ... plus oracle check.
H3 (interaction): ...
Explicit "U not critical" pattern: at fixed L1 and iso-budget, |Δ| < 3 pts with 95% CI within ±5 across all synthetic tasks AND no difference at L3(oracle) → conclude U not critical; the frontier AUC ratio within 5%.
Also "lookup not critical" if ORACLE ≈ SLIDING on multi-evidence tasks.

Diagnostics decision tree per researcher's condition 1 & reading rule ORACLE≈FULL vs ORACLE≪FULL.

Also: report compute table + accuracy per attended token as required when equalization inexact (condition from researcher).

Real benchmarks: InfiniteBench EN-QA + En.MC, LongBench subset (narrativeqa, qasper, hotpotqa, 2wikimqa, musique, dureader?, lcc/repocode), RULER (synthetic, configurable → gives us annotated evidence, n up to large), and an agentic task: long-trace following (synthetic agent log QA with annotated evidence) + maybe "Track-the-state" / multi-session dialogue state tracking. Choose: RULER variants (NIAH-1/2/3, VT, cwe, fwe, qa), InfiniteBench (real), LongBench (real), and a synthetic agentic trace task (ATQA). Justify.

Lengths: 4k/16k/32k primary, 64k/128k stretch on NIAH only (cost).

Also note: model base 32k native max → FULL control capped at 32k unless RoPE scaling; declare.

